      "value": "I just want to amend this a bit. I've since discovered the issue I was having with player shadows was not as simple as shadowmasks vs distance shadowmasks. \nDistance shadowmasks did indeed fix the problem with avatar being lit by the sun in areas that were in baked shadow, but shadowmasks themselves were not the cause of the problem. The problem was some weird interaction with scripts in Bakery where having a Bakery light script for light bounces on the real time Unity directional light would result in light probes baking improperly and failing to account for static shadow casters. Moving the Bakery light script into it's own game object solved the problem and my avatars now cast shadows and receive shadows from other dynamic objects and baked lighting through light probes.\nThat said, Distance Shadowmasks do still have a benefit. \nThe reason I noticed VRChat disabled them was because my formerly sharp lightmaps became blocky within VRChat. This was becuase the lightmaps WERE blocky, but I could not see this in Unity because the Distance Shadowmasks took over for the lightmaps close to the camera, and those render at high resolution. In other words, Distance Shadowmakes can both improve visuals, and enable creators to make worlds smaller, so they download faster, without compromising on quality.",
      "viewerReactions": null
    },
